4.11 Casio (Tokyo, Est. 1957)
The Indestructible Module and The Democratic Tool Revolution
Pivotal Reference Pieces:
G-Shock DW-5000C (1983): The shock-resistant icon engineered by Kikuo Ibe.
Casiotron (1974): The world’s first digital watch with an automatic calendar function.
Key Historical Innovations:
The Hollow-Case Floating Module: The digital quartz core suspended with minimal contact points inside an elastomer resin cradle.
Tough Solar & Wave Ceptor / Multi-Band 6: Self-charging solar cells paired with terrestrial atomic radio clock calibration.
Carbon Core Guard: Modern evolution using carbon-fiber-reinforced polymer for lightweight structural rigidity.
Curatorial Narrative:
In 1981, Casio engineer Kikuo Ibe dropped a mechanical pocket watch gifted by his father, breaking it into pieces. He formed "Project Team Tough" with a single goal: build a watch based on the "Triple 10" criteria (survive a 10-meter drop, endure 10 atmospheres of water pressure, and boast a 10-year battery life). After dropping over 200 prototypes from third-story bathroom windows, Ibe conceived the hollow-structure case inspired by a rubber ball bouncing down the road.
Unique UI/UX Interaction Component (Casio-Inspired):
The 10-Meter Drop & Shock Absorption Visualizer: Users trigger a simulated high-impact kinetic drop onto concrete. The simulation records the $G\text{-force}$ impact vector, demonstrating how the outer polyurethane bezel absorbs the primary compression while the floating quartz module experiences zero damaging shock.
Visual Direction & Imagery Cues (Extracted from Casio.com):
Structural Cross-Section Cue: High-contrast cutaway showing the red urethane shock absorbers, stainless steel inner case, and floating circuit board.
Display Typography Cue: High-legibility 7-segment digital LCD matrix backlit with vibrant emerald electro-luminescence (EL Backlight).
Atmospheric Styling: Matte Shockproof Black (#0B0B0C) and Vivid Utility Amber/Red (#D9381E).

